## Risks

- **Stale list.** A tools change that alters what `replay` or `catalog-version` print without touching the four kept files. Today none exists: `replay`'s shipped code names no other module, and the scan keeps it so. `catalog-version` runs `catalog::run_version`, which reaches only `patches::js` and `patches::repo_root` (both in `patches.rs`, whose `js` module names no crate module). Both files stay on. The scan covers only `replay.rs`, so a change that makes `catalog.rs`'s `run_version` or `newest_version` call another module is not guarded. It is caught only by review of the header's list; the header names that dependence.
- **Scan limits.** Single-line strings only, `#[cfg(test)]` recognised only as the bare attribute line, and `mod x;` not seen. All are documented in the helper's comment. They can only report a false alarm (e.g. the brace count going wrong), never hide a real use, except `mod x;`, which `replay.rs` has none of.
- **Self-timing.** `changes` runs `main`'s copy of the script, so this pull request runs every job. The narrowing applies only to later pull requests.
- **Test fragility.** `ci-scope.test.ts` is slow (dozens of git processes per case). Keep the new cases few as written. The path scans at the end of that file stay green: no non-comment literal under `e2e/` or `apps/web/src` names `crates/tools` (I grepped).
- **Shell.** Keep the new arms plain `case` lines with no comment between arms.

